-- title:
--   π is a non-zero-divisor on W[[u,v]]/(uv-π)
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and let $\pi \in W$ be an element lying in the non-zero-divisors of $W$, i.e. multiplication by $\pi$ on $W$ is injective. Write $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ \pi$ for the quotient of the ring $W[[u,v]]$ of formal power series in two variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\ 2$ by the principal ideal generated by $X_0 X_1 - C(\pi)$, where $C$ denotes the constant-coefficient inclusion $W \to W[[u,v]]$; thus the model is $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$. Write $\mathrm{const}\ \pi\ w$ for the image of the constant series $C(w)$ under the quotient map, so that $\mathrm{const}\ \pi\ \pi$ is the class of $\pi$ in this quotient. The assertion is that $\mathrm{const}\ \pi\ \pi$ belongs to the submonoid of non-zero-divisors of $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ \pi$: for every $x$ in the quotient, $x \cdot \mathrm{const}\ \pi\ \pi = 0$ implies $x = 0$, and likewise $\mathrm{const}\ \pi\ \pi \cdot x = 0$ implies $x = 0$.
--
--   This is the ring-theoretic form, for the explicit local model $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$ of a modular curve at a supersingular point, of flatness in the $\pi$-direction; it also yields that the images of $u$ and $v$ are non-zero-divisors, since their product is $\pi$. It is used in the analysis of deformations of special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules and in the determination of the local structure (regularity, height-one primes, adic completions) of the crossing model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_const_mem_nonZeroDivisors.lean

